; DERIVED - not verbatim. RFC 9679 ships no CDDL, no ABNF, no JSON Schema and
; no formal grammar of any kind: it states the thumbprint input as a numbered
; procedure (Section 3) plus five bullet lists of required parameters
; (Sections 4.1 to 4.5). This file is written by this library from that prose
; and is cross-checked against the one worked encoding the document prints
; (source lines 442 to 444, A4 01 02 20 01 21 58 20 ... 22 58 20 ...).
;
; record:   rfc-9679
; source:   RFC 9679, sha256:f6b460e38ba1496bbba7d1430175f6398af104d6b8803f7a4ea059af5d3fa29d
; written:  2026-10-18 (FX-1 pass 1)
; cite as:  "derived by m-of-n/library from RFC 9679 Sections 3, 4.1-4.5 and 6",
;           never as the RFC's own grammar (R-O-03).

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What this grammar describes
;
; It describes the structure that is HASHED - the output of Section 3 step 1 -
; and NOT a COSE_Key as received. A received COSE_Key (RFC 9052 Section 7) may
; carry kid, alg, key_ops, Base IV and any number of profile parameters; step 1
; constructs a new map holding only the parameters below (R-0007), and step 2
; encodes THAT map under RFC 8949 Section 4.2.1.
;
; CDDL maps are closed: every member of the data map must be matched by an
; entry of the group. That is exactly the Section 4 exclusion rule, so no
; "extra parameters are forbidden" socket is needed or wanted here.
;
; TWO THINGS CDDL CANNOT STATE, both of which this input depends on:
;
;   1. Map entry ORDER. CDDL describes a map as an unordered set of entries.
;      The order of the encoded entries is fixed by RFC 8949 Section 4.2.1
;      (bytewise lexicographic order of the encoded keys), which gives
;      1 (0x01) < -1 (0x20) < -2 (0x21) < -3 (0x22) for every key type below.
;      See thumbprint-computation.derived.json, "entry_order".
;   2. Encoding DETERMINISM: preferred serialization of every head, definite
;      lengths, no indefinite-length strings. Also RFC 8949 Section 4.2.1.
;
; A conforming producer therefore satisfies this grammar AND both rules above.
; Validating against this file alone does not establish that a byte string is
; a well-formed thumbprint input.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

COSE-Key-Thumbprint-Input = OKP-Thumbprint-Input
                          / EC2-Thumbprint-Input
                          / RSA-Thumbprint-Input
                          / Symmetric-Thumbprint-Input
                          / HSS-LMS-Thumbprint-Input

; Section 4.1 - Octet Key Pair, e.g. X25519, Ed25519.
; R-0012 .. R-0015. No d (label -4): an OKP private key and its public key
; have the same thumbprint.
OKP-Thumbprint-Input = {
  1  => 1,            ; "kty", int, OKP            (R-0013)
  -1 => int,          ; "crv"                      (R-0014)
  -2 => bstr          ; "x"                        (R-0015)
}

; Section 4.2 - Elliptic curve keys with x- and y-coordinates, e.g. P-256.
; R-0016 .. R-0024. y is a bstr and never the bool of a compressed point:
; R-0022 requires the uncompressed representation and R-0024 requires a
; compressed point to be converted before the thumbprint is computed.
; No d (label -4): an EC2 private key and its public key have the same
; thumbprint.
EC2-Thumbprint-Input = {
  1  => 2,            ; "kty", int, EC2            (R-0017)
  -1 => int,          ; "crv"                      (R-0018)
  -2 => bstr,         ; "x"                        (R-0019)
  -3 => bstr          ; "y", uncompressed only     (R-0020, R-0022, R-0023)
}

; Section 4.3 - RSA public keys (labels per RFC 8230).
; R-0025 .. R-0028. Public parameters only: none of d, p, q, dP, dQ, qInv or
; other enters. NOTE the defect recorded in README.md: this document states no
; normalisation for these two bstrs, so two encodings of one modulus are two
; thumbprints.
RSA-Thumbprint-Input = {
  1  => 3,            ; "kty", int, RSA            (R-0026)
  -1 => bstr,         ; "n", modulus               (R-0027)
  -2 => bstr          ; "e", public exponent       (R-0028)
}

; Section 4.4 - Symmetric keys.
; R-0029 .. R-0031. The hashed bytes contain the secret itself; Section 7
; (R-0060 .. R-0064) constrains when this may be done at all.
Symmetric-Thumbprint-Input = {
  1  => 4,            ; "kty", int, Symmetric      (R-0030)
  -1 => bstr          ; "k", the key material      (R-0031)
}

; Section 4.5 - HSS-LMS keys (labels per RFC 8778).
; R-0032 .. R-0034. "pub" carries the LMS and LM-OTS typecodes inside itself,
; so for this key type - and no other - the algorithm parameters are inside
; the hashed bytes.
HSS-LMS-Thumbprint-Input = {
  1  => 5,            ; "kty", int, HSS-LMS        (R-0033)
  -1 => bstr          ; "pub", LMS public key      (R-0034)
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Section 4.6 - other key types. The required-parameter set for a key type not
; listed above is defined by that key type's own specification (R-0035), so no
; rule for it can be written here. A record added to the alternation above
; must cite the defining document, not this file.
;
; Section 4 - kty itself. R-0008 requires label 1 in every key type, R-0009
; requires the integer value from the IANA "COSE Key Types" registry, and
; R-0010 removes tstr, which RFC 9052 Section 7.1 would otherwise admit. The
; literal int values above are that rule applied; a tstr kty cannot be written
; in this grammar.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
